    "texte": [
      "Across the history of modern thought, humanity has endured a succession of humbling 'Copernican wounds' to its cosmic narcissism. Copernicus displaced the Earth from the geometric centre of the universe; Darwin revealed our biological kinship with all terrestrial life; and Freud uncovered the unconscious forces beneath rational self-transparency. Yet throughout each of those revolutions, one sanctuary of human exceptionalism appeared inviolable: our monopoly upon higher-order reasoning, linguistic eloquence, artistic composition, and strategic synthesis. Today, the rapid ascent of frontier artificial intelligence—capable of generating fluent prose, proving mathematical theorems, diagnosing medical imaging, and composing symphonic variations within seconds—has triggered an acute crisis of human uniqueness.",
      "More than three centuries ago, Blaise Pascal anticipated the essential fault line dividing mechanical calculation from lived human intelligence in his famous distinction between the 'esprit de géométrie'—the formal, rule-bound deduction of logical sequences—and the 'esprit de finesse', the intuitive, embodied sensibility that grasps subtle moral nuances, unspoken grief, and contextual ambiguity. Contemporary large language models and neural networks represent an astonishing triumph of statistical geometry, predicting patterns across trillions of tokens without ever experiencing hunger, mortality, joy, or moral remorse.",
      "The philosopher Hubert Dreyfus long ago demonstrated in 'What Computers Still Can't Do' that human intelligence is never a disembodied software program running inside a cranial hard drive: our understanding of the world is rooted in our fragile, mortal bodies, our emotional attachments, and our shared cultural practices. A machine can simulate the syntactic surface of a love poem or a legal plea with uncanny fluency, yet it inhabits no biographical stakes in the words it assembles.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe the breathless metaphysical confusion with which certain technology executives proclaim that machines have achieved 'human-level understanding' the moment a chatbot passes a standardised bar exam—while simultaneously designing human workplaces to force flesh-and-blood employees to behave like predictable, emotionless algorithms.",
      "As the computer scientist Joseph Weizenbaum warned in 'Computer Power and Human Reason' after observing how eagerly users confided intimate secrets to his rudimentary 1966 program ELIZA, the gravest danger of artificial intelligence lies not in machines becoming genuinely human, but in human beings progressively lowering their own standards of empathy, judgement, and dignity until we mistake statistical mimicry for authentic consciousness.",
