"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Fellows of the Academies of Science, Ladies and Gentlemen, for nearly four centuries following Francis Bacon's declaration that 'knowledge itself is power', modern science marched forward under a radiant moral assumption: that every expansion of humanity's empirical mastery over nature would automatically advance human enlightenment, material security, and peace. Today, standing amidst the convergence of thermonuclear arsenals, synthetic pathogen engineering, germline gene editing, geoengineering interventions, and autonomous weapons systems, that comforting innocence has vanished forever. Before this assembly, a sobering question confronts the global scientific community: when our technical capabilities outstrip our institutional wisdom, what does genuine scientific responsibility demand?",
"In July 1945, watching the first nuclear detonation illuminate the New Mexico desert at Alamogordo, J. Robert Oppenheimer famously recalled the ancient scripture—'Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds'—marking the exact historical instant when physics lost its moral insulation. Ten years later, in the Russell-Einstein Manifesto of 1955, Bertrand Russell and Albert Einstein issued an urgent appeal to scientists across ideological divides to remember their shared humanity above all national allegiances and renounce technologies capable of extinguishing human civilisation.",
"Yet the dominant institutional culture of contemporary research still clings to a comforting myth: the doctrine of 'value-neutral science'. According to this convenient division of labour, scientists are responsible solely for discovering how nature works and building technically elegant tools, while the ethical and political consequences of deploying those tools belong exclusively to politicians, corporations, or military commanders.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the selective modesty with which certain frontier technology laboratories invoke this doctrine of neutrality today: when pitching multibillion-dollar investments to venture capitalists or governments, researchers boast grandiosely that their breakthroughs will 'transform civilisation and reshape humanity'; yet the moment ethicists or citizens question the catastrophic risks of unchecked deployment, those exact same researchers suddenly claim to be humble, apolitical technicians merely following mathematical curiosity.",
"As the philosopher Hans Jonas argued in 'The Imperative of Responsibility', when scientific research itself becomes inseparable from industrial and military power, the scientist can no longer hide behind pure theoretical curiosity: foresight, precaution, and public whistleblowing become intrinsic duties of scientific integrity.",
"Insisting upon heightened scientific responsibility does not mean, Ladies and Gentlemen, stifling free inquiry, imposing ideological censorship on laboratories, or retreating into anti-scientific obscurantism. Curiosity-driven basic research remains one of the noblest expressions of the human spirit.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call upon academies, universities, and governments to institutionalise a new covenant of scientific responsibility: embedding rigorous ethical assessment, biosafety, and existential risk review into every stage of frontier research funding; protecting scientists and engineers through robust international whistleblower safeguards when they alert the public to hazardous dual-use technologies; and cultivating an education in which every future scientist is trained not merely as a brilliant specialist, but as a morally accountable guardian of human dignity and our fragile biosphere."
