"texte": [
"Convening at the Karolinska Institutet in Stockholm for a public symposium on bioethics and future medicine, the clinical geneticist Professor Astrid Lindholm and the philosopher of medicine Dr Julian Vance confronted one of the most delicate conceptual and moral frontiers of contemporary science: can societies maintain a coherent boundary between therapeutic medicine—which seeks to cure disease and restore health—and human augmentation, which aims to engineer cognitive, physical, or genetic traits beyond species-typical norms? 'Every day in clinical practice, technologies such as CRISPR gene editing, neural-computer interfaces, and targeted neuropharmacology demonstrate miraculous potential to alleviate inherited blindness, paralysis, and neurodegenerative illness,' Professor Lindholm began. 'Yet the exact same molecular tools capable of repairing a lethal genetic mutation can also be deployed to enhance memory, muscle endurance, or embryonic traits—making a rigid separation between therapy and enhancement scientifically elusive.'",
"'You highlight the technical overlap of the tools with undeniable precision, Astrid, yet abandoning the moral distinction between therapy and enhancement carries profound ethical peril,' Dr Vance replied with incisive gravity. 'As the philosopher Jürgen Habermas argued in "The Future of Human Nature", healing an illness restores a person's capacity to act as an autonomous equal, whereas genetically programming an unborn child according to parental consumer preferences introduces an irreversible asymmetry—turning a future free citizen into the manufactured product of another's design.'",
"'I share your opposition to authoritarian eugenics and consumer designer babies, Julian, yet we should beware of treating current human biology as an untouchable sacred ceiling,' Lindholm countered, refining her clinical perspective. 'After all, vaccines, eyeglasses, literacy, and cognitive prosthetics already enhance human capacities far beyond our ancestral evolutionary baseline without destroying moral equality.'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe the selective rhetoric with which commercial bio-enhancement clinics market luxury genetic and cognitive augmentation today,' Vance remarked with a dry smile: 'procedures accessible exclusively to ultra-wealthy clients seeking competitive advantages for their offspring are solemnly promoted under the compassionate banner of "preventive healthcare" and "democratising human potential".'",
"'That market distortion points straight to the core social danger identified by Michael Sandel in "The Case against Perfection",' Lindholm conceded thoughtfully. 'If expensive cognitive or genetic enhancements become market commodities, existing socioeconomic inequalities risk hardening into biological castes—while parents feel pressured into an exhausting biomedical arms race merely to keep their children competitive.'",
"'Precisely!' Vance agreed, welcoming the synthesis. 'As the physician-philosopher Georges Canguilhem demonstrated in "The Normal and the Pathological", health is never a static statistical standard imposed by market competition, but an organism's living capacity to adapt and flourish in solidarity with others. Drawing a workable boundary requires democratic bioethical governance rather than laissez-faire commercialisation.'",
"Astrid Lindholm nodded in agreement: 'In short, navigating the shifting frontier between therapy and augmentation demands uniting scientific humility with democratic justice: guaranteeing universal, equitable access to genuine medical therapies that relieve suffering while prohibiting commercial heritable enhancement and competitive biological stratification—so that medicine remains an art of healing and solidarity rather than an engine of inequality.'"
],
