"texte": [
"INTERVIEWER: Dr Henrik Lindqvist, scholar of digital geopolitics and critical infrastructure governance, contemporary public debate often treats the digital sphere as an ethereal 'cloud' floating effortlessly above geography and politics. Why has the extreme concentration of physical and computational infrastructure—subsea fibre-optic cables, hyperscale cloud data centres, semiconductor foundries, and low-Earth-orbit satellite constellations—turned 'digital sovereignty' into one of the defining strategic questions of the twenty-first century?",
"DR LINDQVIST: Because the 'cloud' is in reality a vast, capital-intensive physical empire concentrated in the hands of a tiny oligopoly of states and corporations! Today, roughly ninety-eight percent of intercontinental internet traffic travels through subsea cables, a handful of hyperscale cloud providers host the critical administrative, health, and financial data of entire nations, and semiconductor fabrication rests upon fragile geographic chokepoints. When a country depends entirely upon foreign-controlled infrastructure for its communications, public administration, and artificial intelligence compute, its nominal political sovereignty becomes perilously conditional.",
"INTERVIEWER: Yet critics of 'digital sovereignty' frequently warn that the concept is exploited by authoritarian governments to justify internet censorship, data localisation walls, and the fragmentation of the open global web into a 'splinternet'. How can democracies pursue digital autonomy without sliding into protectionist nationalism?",
"DR LINDQVIST: That distinction is absolutely vital! Authoritarian 'cyber-sovereignty' seeks to erect digital walls to control what citizens can read, say, or think; democratic 'digital sovereignty', by contrast, means ensuring that citizens and elected institutions possess genuine self-determination over their data, critical infrastructure, and algorithmic rules—so that neither an authoritarian state nor an unaccountable foreign tech monopoly can unilaterally switch off vital services or extract citizen data without democratic consent.",
"INTERVIEWER: In practice, however, many governments proclaim ambitious 'sovereign cloud' initiatives only to sign multibillion-dollar contracts licensing proprietary software from the very same global tech giants they claimed to become independent from. Why does genuine infrastructural autonomy prove so elusive?",
"DR LINDQVIST: Because true sovereignty cannot be bought as a rebranded commercial license! When states underinvest in long-term public research, open-source software ecosystems, and cooperative regional compute infrastructure, 'sovereign cloud' initiatives become mere cosmetic wrappers around foreign proprietary architectures—creating deep technical lock-in disguised behind national flags.",
"INTERVIEWER: What realistic strategic pathway would allow medium-sized nations and regional coalitions—across Europe, Africa, Latin America, and Asia—to build authentic digital sovereignty without wasteful duplication?",
"DR LINDQVIST: By pooling resources through open, interoperable digital public goods: investing jointly in regional open-source cloud and high-performance compute consortia; mandating open technical standards and data portability so public administrations are never trapped by a single vendor; diversifying subsea cable routes and satellite connectivity; and treating core digital architectures—identity systems, payment rails, and public AI models—as shared civic infrastructure governed transparently under democratic law."
