"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Scholars and Guests, Ladies and Gentlemen, standing at the threshold of an era defined by artificial intelligence, genetic engineering, interplanetary probes, and instantaneous planetary communication, humanity possesses technical instruments that would have appeared miraculous to every preceding age. Yet amidst this dazzling cascade of innovation, a foundational philosophical question—at once ancient and urgently contemporary—demands our collective honesty: does rapid technological progress necessarily constitute genuine civilisational progress? To confuse the growing sophistication of our tools with the moral, ecological, and spiritual elevation of our species is perhaps the most seductive—and perilous—illusion of modernity.",
"As early as 1750, in his famous 'Discourse on the Sciences and Arts', Jean-Jacques Rousseau startled Enlightenment Europe by questioning whether the refinement of technical arts and sciences had genuinely purified human morals or merely draped sophisticated chains of vanity and inequality over human freedom. Two centuries later, witnessing the twentieth century harness advanced chemistry, industrial logistics, and nuclear physics for unprecedented mechanized slaughter, the historian Lewis Mumford warned in 'Technics and Civilization' that technical power divorced from humane values produces not a higher civilisation, but 'technological barbarism'.",
"True civilisational progress cannot be measured merely by processor speeds, orbital launches, or gross industrial output; it must be judged by qualitative ethical criteria: whether a society expands justice for the vulnerable, deepens democratic self-government, nurtures artistic and philosophical wonder, and sustains harmonious reciprocity with the living Earth.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the self-congratulatory narrative by which hypermodern societies equate every trivial gadget upgrade with historical advancement: a civilisation capable of streaming high-definition entertainment onto pocket glass screens while allowing millions of children to suffer preventable malnutrition, social loneliness to reach epidemic levels, and the Earth's climate systems to destabilise can scarcely claim to have solved the riddle of human flourishing.",
"In his philosophy of 'Reverence for Life', the physician, musician, and Nobel laureate Albert Schweitzer offered an enduring diagnosis of modern imbalance: our technical capacity to manipulate matter has raced ahead like a runaway locomotive, while our ethical reflection on the purpose of human existence has lagged dangerously behind. Authentic civilisation begins only when moral wisdom overtakes technical power and directs it toward life-affirming ends.",
"To distinguish technological acceleration from civilisational progress is not, Ladies and Gentlemen, to despise human ingenuity or yearn nostalgically for pre-modern scarcity. Clean water engineering, antibiotics, renewable energy, and global literacy are among the finest triumphs of human creativity when guided by justice.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call upon scientists, educators, policymakers, and citizens to reclaim the true measure of civilisational greatness: subordinating technological innovation to democratic deliberation, ecological stewardship, and universal human dignity; investing as passionately in philosophy, ethics, the arts, and civic solidarity as we invest in engineering; and affirming together that humanity achieves genuine progress not when our machines grow infinitely clever, but when our societies grow wiser, fairer, and more compassionate toward all life upon this shared Earth."
