    "texte": [
      "For more than two centuries, industrial civilisation narrated its own ascent as an unbroken epic of human emancipation from natural necessity. Powered by fossilised sunlight—coal, petroleum, and natural gas—modern societies erected gleaming metropolises, multiplied agricultural yields, and extended human longevity across continents. Yet as accelerating climate destabilisation, collapsing insect and vertebrate populations, depleted aquifers, and degraded topsoils converge into a systemic ecological crisis, contemporary humanity confronts an inescapable truth: ecological collapse is not an accidental technical malfunction to be patched at the margins, but an existential revelation exposing the deepest structural contradictions of industrial civilisation itself.",
      "As early as the nineteenth century, Karl Marx diagnosed what ecological sociologists today call the 'metabolic rift': by concentrating populations in industrial cities and subjecting agriculture and manufacturing to short-term profit cycles, capitalist industrialism systematically interrupted the natural nutrient cycles that replenish soil and water—robbing both the worker and the Earth of their regenerative vitality.",
      "A century later, in 'Ecology as Politics', the philosopher André Gorz sharpened this critique by exposing the self-defeating logic of industrial productivism: an economic system whose survival depends upon endless material throughput inevitably treats the living biosphere as an infinite quarry from which to extract raw inputs and an infinite sewer into which to dump toxic waste—until the mounting cost of repairing ecological damage begins to exceed the wealth generated by growth itself.",
