"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Delegates, Ladies and Gentlemen, climate change is frequently described in diplomatic communiqués as a universal threat affecting all of humanity alike. While it is physically true that greenhouse gases respect no national frontiers, treating climate disruption as a symmetrical crisis shared equally by all nations conceals a profound moral and historical injustice. Over the past century and a half, the overwhelming majority of cumulative carbon emissions trapped in the Earth's atmosphere originated from the industrialisation of a small group of wealthy major powers—yet the most devastating consequences of rising seas, prolonged droughts, lethal heatwaves, and superstorms fall disproportionately upon vulnerable nations in the Global South that contributed least to causing the emergency.",
"As the moral philosopher Henry Shue demonstrated in his foundational distinction between 'subsistence emissions' and 'luxury emissions', ethical climate governance cannot treat every tonne of carbon as morally equivalent. Asking a developing nation to forego basic electrification, clean cooking, or rural clinics while wealthy nations defend high-emission lifestyles, SUV fleets, and frequent aviation is not environmental stewardship—it is ecological colonialism.",
"Under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, the international community formally recognised this ethical asymmetry through the principle of 'Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities'. Because major industrialised powers consumed the lion's share of the global atmospheric carbon budget to build their present prosperity, they bear an inescapable historical debt to lead rapid domestic decarbonisation and finance adaptation and clean technology transfer elsewhere.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the selective accounting practised by wealthy emitter nations during international climate negotiations today: after lecturing developing countries for decades on the sacred sanctity of contractual debt repayment down to the last cent, major powers suddenly plead 'budgetary constraints' when asked to honour their modest, long-overdue pledges for climate adaptation and 'loss and damage' compensation.",
"Furthermore, the climate justice crisis is compounded by asymmetric financial architecture: burdened by high-interest sovereign debt denominated in foreign currencies, many climate-vulnerable states are forced to spend more each year servicing foreign creditors—and even expanding fossil-fuel or mineral exports to earn hard currency—than they can invest in flood defences, resilient agriculture, or solar grids.",
"To insist upon the historical responsibilities of major powers is not, Ladies and Gentlemen, to absolve emerging economies or domestic elites anywhere from pursuing clean, sustainable pathways. Every nation shares a duty to protect our common atmosphere according to its means.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call upon major industrialised powers to honour climate justice not as discretionary charity, but as binding historical restitution: accelerating domestic fossil-fuel phase-outs on a science-aligned timeline; delivering substantial, grant-based climate finance and debt cancellation for vulnerable nations rather than predatory loans; establishing open-access licensing for essential clean-energy technologies; and recognising that humanity will never stabilise the Earth's climate unless ecological ambition is anchored firmly in international justice."
