    "texte": [
      "For more than a century, global geopolitics has been written in the black ink of hydrocarbons. From naval coal stations to Middle Eastern oilfields and Eurasian gas pipelines, control over fossil-fuel extraction and transit chokepoints dictated alliances, triggered wars, and shaped the rise and fall of great powers. Today, driven by the existential imperative to decarbonise the global economy before climate tipping points become irreversible, humanity has embarked upon the most rapid energy transformation in history. Yet as an observer of contemporary energy geopolitics, I am struck by a sobering paradox: while replacing fossil fuels with solar panels, wind turbines, and electric batteries is indispensable for planetary survival, the energy transition does not abolish geopolitical dependency—it replaces old hydrocarbon vulnerabilities with fierce new rivalries over critical minerals, refining monopolies, and clean-technology supply chains.",
      "As the energy historian Daniel Yergin has observed in 'The New Map', transitioning from a fossil-fuel system to a renewable-electrification system fundamentally shifts power from countries blessed with subterranean oil and gas reserves to states that control lithium, cobalt, nickel, copper, and rare-earth elements—and, even more decisively, the industrial capacity to refine those ores into battery-grade chemicals and high-efficiency solar wafers.",
      "Equally sobering is the physical realism emphasized by the energy scholar Vaclav Smil: energy transitions are never effortless software updates, but massive material undertakings requiring unprecedented quantities of mined metals, grid copper, and high-voltage transmission infrastructure—meaning that a poorly planned transition can inadvertently generate acute local ecological destruction and social conflict around mining sites.",
