"texte": [
"Convening at the Stockholm Environment Institute for a public debate on sustainable economics, the green-growth economist Professor Jonathan Sterling and the ecological systems scientist Dr Maya Lindholm confronted one of the central empirical and philosophical disputes of our time: can modern economies genuinely 'decouple' continuous Gross Domestic Product growth from material resource extraction and ecosystem depletion? 'Over the past two decades, more than thirty industrialised economies have successfully expanded their GDP while reducing domestic territorial carbon emissions through renewable energy, LED lighting, and energy-efficiency breakthroughs,' Professor Sterling opened. 'As neoclassical growth theorists such as Robert Solow argued, human ingenuity and technological substitution continually overcome resource bottlenecks—proving that smart, dematerialised growth can reconcile prosperity with planetary protection.'",
"'You cite selective territorial carbon statistics with impressive fluency, Jonathan, yet those figures conceal a profound biophysical illusion,' Dr Lindholm replied with incisive precision. 'First, much of the apparent emission drop in wealthy nations stems from offshoring heavy manufacturing to Asia while importing the finished goods; second, as ecological economists such as Jason Hickel and Tim Jackson have demonstrated empirically, there is zero evidence anywhere on Earth of sufficiently rapid "absolute decoupling" of global GDP from total material extraction, biodiversity loss, and chemical pollution.'",
"'I readily acknowledge that offshoring emissions is accounting sleight of hand rather than genuine progress, Maya, and that efficiency gains alone can trigger the "Jevons paradox"—where cheaper energy encourages greater total consumption,' Sterling countered, refining his institutional stance. 'Yet with strict carbon pricing, circular-economy recycling mandates, and clean electrification, surely market innovation can bend the resource curve downward without forcing societies into painful economic contraction?'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe the semantic magic by which green-growth enthusiasts promise "dematerialised economic expansion" today,' Lindholm remarked with a dry smile: 'economists announce that digital services and software algorithms weigh nothing at all—conveniently forgetting the colossal physical footprint of rare-earth mining, hyperscale data centres, transoceanic fibre cables, and power grids required to run every single cloud computation.'",
"'That physical grounding is an indispensable corrective to naive digital optimism, Maya!' Sterling conceded thoughtfully. 'Whether one calls the goal post-growth wellbeing or qualitative green development, our current obsession with indiscriminate quantitative output clearly fails to distinguish life-enhancing sectors like education and ecosystem restoration from destructive fossil and disposable consumption.'",
"'Precisely!' Lindholm agreed, welcoming the convergence. 'Escaping ecosystem depletion requires abandoning the dogma of blind aggregate growth in favour of selective sufficiency: intentionally expanding clean energy, public healthcare, education, and regenerative agriculture while deliberately scaling down ecologically destructive sectors—from fossil fuels and fast fashion to private aviation and planned obsolescence.'",
"Jonathan Sterling nodded in agreement: 'In short, human societies cannot rely upon magical technological decoupling to rescue a growth-addicted economy; only democratic policies that cap total material throughput within planetary boundaries while redirecting innovation toward human flourishing can reconcile economic vitality with a living Earth.'"
