    "texte": [
      "Common sense habitually conceives of language as a transparent windowpane through which human minds observe and describe an already finished external world. Yet across philosophy, linguistics, and political sociology, a far more unsettling truth has steadily emerged: far from merely mirroring an objective reality that precedes it, language actively categorises, frames, and constructs what a society perceives as natural, legitimate, or unthinkable. Every vocabulary embeds a silent metaphysics and a subtle distribution of authority—making control over words, definitions, and public idioms one of the most decisive instruments of political and social power.",
      "In his classic 1946 essay 'Politics and the English Language', George Orwell exposed with surgical precision how degraded political prose is engineered to defend the indefensible: by replacing concrete, vivid verbs with passive Latinate abstractions—calling aerial bombardment 'pacification', mass displacement 'transfer of population', or brutal austerity 'fiscal consolidation'—power anaesthetises moral empathy and prevents citizens from picturing the human suffering behind administrative decrees.",
      "The sociologist Pierre Bourdieu deepened this insight in 'Language and Symbolic Power', showing that linguistic authority never resides solely inside dictionary definitions, but in the institutional legitimacy of the speaker. Through 'symbolic violence', dominant classes impose their own dialect, stylistic register, and conceptual categories as the sole standard of 'rational objectivity', quietly disqualifying the lived experience and vernacular voices of marginalised citizens before a single argument is even heard.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe the euphemistic ballet performed by contemporary corporate and political communications teams whenever unpopular measures are unveiled: workers are never fired, they undergo 'proactive workforce right-sizing'; toxic ecological destruction becomes 'environmental remediation opportunities'; and pervasive digital surveillance is marketed affectionately as 'personalised user-experience optimisation'.",
      "During the darkest years of twentieth-century totalitarianism, the philologist Victor Klemperer documented in 'LTI: The Language of the Third Reich' how ideological regimes infiltrate everyday speech through seemingly trivial catchphrases and mechanical metaphors until ordinary citizens unconsciously think inside the regime's conceptual cage. Klemperer observed that words can act like tiny doses of arsenic: swallowed unnoticed, they gradually poison moral judgement from within.",
      "To recognise that language constructs social reality and serves power is not, however, to surrender to linguistic determinism or claim that objective truth is merely an arbitrary word-game. Precisely because language is open, polysemous, and continually contested, words also remain humanity's sharpest instrument of emancipation.",
      "Reclaiming linguistic agency in an era of spin and algorithmic slogans therefore requires cultivating vigilant lexical hygiene across civic life: insisting upon concrete, accountable verbs in public law and journalism; safeguarding independent literature, philosophy, and humanities education where citizens learn to dissect euphemisms; and remembering that a free society begins whenever people refuse borrowed clichés and dare to name reality with clarity, precision, and moral courage."
