    "texte": [
      "At first glance, hyperconnected societies appear saturated with shared stories, viral images, and instantaneous global references. Billions of citizens carry portals to the entire archive of human cinema, literature, and news inside their pockets, linked together across continents in real time. Yet beneath this dazzling surface of perpetual digital connectivity lies a profound sociological paradox: even as technical connection reaches ubiquity, the 'collective imaginary'—the deep reservoir of shared myths, civic symbols, historical narratives, and common future horizons that enables a society to imagine itself as a coherent moral community—is quietly disintegrating into millions of algorithmically customised micro-bubbles.",
      "In his classic study 'Imagined Communities', the political scientist Benedict Anderson demonstrated how the rise of print capitalism—shared morning newspapers and vernacular novels—enabled millions of strangers who would never meet face-to-face to experience themselves as co-inhabitants of a common civic time and space. Today, personalised recommendation engines have inverted Anderson's unifying dynamic: rather than gathering citizens around a shared public square of stories and debates, platform algorithms serve each user a hyper-individualised stream tailored to isolate and reinforce their private anxieties, tastes, and prejudices.",
      "As the philosopher Cornelius Castoriadis argued in 'The Imaginary Institution of Society', no human polity can sustain democratic solidarity or long-term collective action through administrative rules and market incentives alone; every society depends upon an 'instituting social imaginary' capable of projecting shared ideals of justice, beauty, and mutual responsibility across generations.",
