"texte": [
"Convening at the International Centre for Literary Translation in Arles for a public symposium on world literature and cross-cultural ethics, the literary translator Dr Sofia Castellano and the philosopher of language Professor Julian Mercer confronted an enduring paradox at the heart of human communication: why does translation remain indispensable to civilisation even though perfect semantic equivalence between languages is strictly impossible? 'Every language carves up human experience according to its own historical memory, syntactic rhythm, and web of cultural connotations,' Dr Castellano opened. 'When a translator renders a lyric poem, a legal statute, or a philosophical treatise from Arabic, Japanese, or Russian into English, something subtle inevitably resists direct transfer—confirming the old Italian adage "traduttore, traditore", translator, traitor.'",
"'You articulate the irreducible singularity of each tongue with undeniable sensitivity, Sofia, yet treating translation merely as a tragic loss risks trapping cultures inside hermetically sealed monads,' Professor Mercer replied with measured precision. 'In his seminal 1923 essay "The Task of the Translator", Walter Benjamin argued that translation is never a sterile copy striving to imitate an original identically; rather, it is an organic "afterlife" through which a literary work matures, reveals latent resonances, and enriches the receiving language itself.'",
"'I gladly endorse Benjamin's vision of creative afterlife, Julian, provided we respect the ethical tension between domesticating a foreign text and preserving its strangeness,' Castellano countered, refining her craft perspective. 'As the translation theorist Lawrence Venuti demonstrated, dominant global languages such as English frequently practise aggressive "domestication"—smoothing away every unfamiliar cultural idiom until foreign masterpieces sound as though they had been written effortlessly in London or New York.'",
