"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Scholars and Guardians of Living Languages, Ladies and Gentlemen, linguists estimate that roughly half of the seven thousand languages currently spoken across our planet risk falling silent before the end of this century—vanishing at an average rate of one ancestral tongue every fortnight. Driven by hyper-globalised media monopolies, uniform digital interfaces, educational centralisation, and economic pressure favouring a handful of dominant global idioms, contemporary cultural homogenisation is silently eroding humanity's richest intangible heritage. Before this assembly, we must confront the true gravity of this quiet extinction: a language is never merely an interchangeable technical code for transmitting commercial data; it is a unique irreplaceable lens through which a human community perceives kinship, ecological wisdom, memory, and the sacred.",
"In his landmark treatise 'Decolonising the Mind', the Kenyan novelist and thinker Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o illuminated why linguistic domination strikes at the very soul of a people: language carries culture, and culture carries—particularly through orature and literature—the entire body of values by which we come to perceive ourselves and our place in the universe. To sever a child from their mother tongue in the classroom or digital square is to alienate them from their own historical memory and creative self-confidence.",
"Equally profound is the Martinican poet and philosopher Édouard Glissant's vision of the 'Tout-Monde': Glissant warned that standardisation into a single global monoculture impoverishes humanity just as severely as biological monoculture devastates a rainforest. True planetary universality does not require erasing local languages in favour of one imperial lingua franca; it flourishes through 'creolisation' and polyphonic relation among intact, self-respecting cultural identities.",
