"texte": [
"Convening at the Marc Bloch Centre in Berlin for a public symposium on history, justice, and democratic reconciliation, the historian of collective memory Professor Henrik Lindholm and the philosopher of law Dr Nadia Benjelloun confronted one of the most delicate dilemmas of contemporary political life: how can societies honour historical truth and the memory of past injustices without allowing memory to be weaponised for partisan vengeance or erased into convenient oblivion? 'Every human community constructs its identity around shared narratives of the past,' Professor Lindholm began. 'As the sociologist Maurice Halbwachs demonstrated in 'On Collective Memory', memory is never a passive photographic archive; it is an active social reconstruction shaped by the frameworks, anxieties, and power relations of the present.'",
"'You capture the constructed nature of collective memory with undeniable clarity, Henrik, yet when political actors treat the past purely as malleable clay to serve present agendas, historical truth itself is imperilled,' Dr Benjelloun replied with incisive gravity. 'As the historian Eric Hobsbawm warned in 'The Invention of Tradition', nationalist myth-makers routinely fabricate heroic lineages and selective grievances—erasing inconvenient chapters of oppression or complicity in order to sanctify current political power.'",
"'I share your vigilance against partisan myth-making, Nadia, which is why critical historiography must remain rigorously anchored in archival evidence and methodological honesty,' Lindholm countered, refining his scholarly stance. 'Yet we must equally beware of the opposite pathology: enforced state amnesia—where post-conflict or post-colonial regimes demand that victims simply 'turn the page' and forget systemic crimes in the name of superficial national harmony.'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe the selective commemorative piety displayed by certain political leaders during national remembrance ceremonies today,' Benjelloun remarked with a dry smile: 'statesmen solemnly lay wreaths at monuments honouring historic struggles against tyranny and slavery while simultaneously cutting funding for public archives, censoring school history curricula that mention uncomfortable national crimes, and demonising contemporary refugees fleeing the very geopolitical disorders those histories helped produce.'",
"'That contradiction highlights why the philosopher Paul Ricœur devoted 'Memory, History, Forgetting' to articulating an ethics of 'just memory',' Lindholm agreed warmly. 'Ricœur showed that healthy societies must steer between two destructive extremes: on one side, an obsessive repetition of trauma that locks communities into permanent victimhood and hatred; on the other, a self-serving oblivion that denies victims recognition and invites the repetition of past atrocities.'",
"'Precisely!' Benjelloun added, completing the synthesis. 'And as Tzvetan Todorov argued in 'Hope and Memory', historical remembrance becomes truly emancipatory only when it moves from literal fixation on one's own group's suffering toward exemplary memory—transforming knowledge of past injustice into a universal ethical commitment to defend the rights of all vulnerable human beings today.'",
"Henrik Lindholm nodded in agreement: 'In short, democratic societies must refuse both politically manipulated myth and cowardly oblivion—sustaining independent historical scholarship, pluralistic public memorials, and courageous civic education so that memory serves truth, reconciliation, and shared human dignity.'"
