"texte": [
"Standing inside the quiet cloister of Santa Croce in Florence on a crisp November afternoon while late sunlight slanted across fourteenth-century frescoes and marble tombs, the literary historian and essayist Dr Lorenzo Bellini paused before a commemorative cenotaph inscribed with the name of Dante Alighieri—the Florentine poet exiled from his native city in 1302 who composed the 'Divine Comedy' amid the fierce municipal feuds, papal intrigues, and theological controversies of medieval Italy. Contemplating how a poem saturated with fourteenth-century Tuscan politics still moves readers across Tokyo, Buenos Aires, and Lagos seven centuries later, Lorenzo reflected upon one of the enduring questions of literary aesthetics: can a literary work truly escape its historical context, or does its universal resonance emerge precisely through—rather than in spite of—its deep historical rootedness?",
"Across twentieth-century literary theory, two opposing absolutisms repeatedly collided over this question. On one side, rigid historicism and vulgar sociological determinism reduced great works of art to passive ideological documents—mere reflections of their author's class position, imperial era, or political prejudices, stripped of any capacity to speak beyond their moment of origin. On the other side, formalist 'art for art's sake' purism treated masterpieces as timeless, self-contained crystals floating serenely above history, politics, and material suffering.",
"In 'Truth and Method', the philosopher Hans-Georg Gadamer dissolved this false dichotomy through his concept of the 'fusion of horizons'. Every literary work is undeniably born within a specific historical horizon—shaped by the language, anxieties, and blind spots of its age—yet a profound work is never a sealed historical capsule: as each new generation reads it from its own historical horizon, a living dialogue unfolds in which the text illuminates our present while our present uncovers unforeseen meanings within the text.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the two symmetrical forms of critical myopia that afflict contemporary debates over classic literature today: on one flank, moralising presentists demand that works written five hundred years ago be purged or dismissed whenever their characters fail to conform to twenty-first-century social norms; on the other flank, nostalgic aesthetes insist that reading a colonial or patriarchal classic requires blinding oneself completely to its historical injustices.",
"As the Russian literary theorist Mikhail Bakhtin demonstrated through his concept of 'great time', masterpieces outlive their immediate epoch not because they float free of history, but because they absorb the deepest polyphonic tensions, linguistic struggles, and existential dilemmas of their own moment so intensely that future ages continue discovering living answers inside them.",
"To affirm that a literary work carries transhistorical resonance is therefore never to excuse its author's historical prejudices or ignore the power relations embedded within its creation. Honest critical reading holds both truths together: situating a work rigorously within its historical world while remaining receptive to its enduring human insight.",
"As twilight deepened over the Arno valley and the bronze bells of Florence tolled across the darkening rooftops, Lorenzo stepped out onto the piazza with a quiet sense of gratitude: a great literary work neither erases its historical birthmark nor remains imprisoned inside it—like a star whose ancient light crosses centuries of darkness to warm a distant window, it endures precisely because it knew how to inhabit its own brief hour on Earth with total fidelity and imaginative courage."
