"texte": [
"Convening at the New School for Social Research in New York for a public dialogue on sociology and subjectivity, the social psychologist Professor David Lindholm and the philosopher Dr Nadia El-Mansouri confronted a foundational paradox of human selfhood: how can an individual be simultaneously a product of internalised social norms and an autonomous moral agent capable of questioning and transforming those very norms? 'From the moment an infant enters the world, human consciousness develops through language, family expectations, school rituals, and cultural scripts,' Professor Lindholm began. 'As George Herbert Mead demonstrated in 'Mind, Self, and Society', the human 'self' is never a solitary island preceding social interaction; rather, we forge our identity by internalising the 'generalised other'—learning to see and judge ourselves through the eyes of our community.'",
"'You capture the social genesis of the self with undeniable clarity, David, yet if identity were nothing more than the passive imprint of social expectations, moral innovation and emancipatory dissent would be inexplicable,' Dr El-Mansouri replied with incisive precision. 'Even within Mead's framework, the socialised 'Me' stands in constant creative tension with the spontaneous, unpredictable 'I'—the reflexive capacity of consciousness to step back, interrogate inherited norms, and refuse roles that violate human dignity.'",
"'I gladly recognise that creative tension, Nadia, though Erving Goffman's dramaturgical sociology in 'The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life' reminds us just how pervasive social scripting remains,' Lindholm countered, refining his sociological lens. 'Across workplaces, family gatherings, and digital profiles, we constantly perform curated roles tailored to audience expectations—so deeply that the boundary between our social mask and our authentic core becomes remarkably porous.'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe the performative conformity of contemporary digital 'authenticity culture',' El-Mansouri remarked with a dry smile: 'millions of social-media users meticulously stage spontaneous, unfiltered confessions using identical lighting, identical therapeutic buzzwords, and identical self-branding formulas—turning the very declaration 'I refuse to conform to society's expectations' into one of the most rigidly scripted performances of hypermodernity.'",
"'That observation illustrates Judith Butler's insight into 'performativity' with uncanny precision, Nadia!' Lindholm agreed warmly. 'Identity is never a fixed, pre-social essence hidden inside us like a stone, nor is it an arbitrary costume we can shed effortlessly at will; it is an ongoing practice enacted through repeated social norms—which nevertheless contain subtle fissures where parody, critique, and reinterpretation can open new possibilities of freedom.'",
"'Precisely!' El-Mansouri added, completing the synthesis. 'Genuine individual autonomy does not consist in the impossible fantasy of escaping all social norms—which would leave us speechless and unmoored—but in moving from unconscious, unexamined conformity to reflective, democratic authorship of the norms we endorse.'",
"David Lindholm nodded in agreement: 'In short, human identity is neither a puppet controlled by society nor a sovereign bubble detached from community; it is an ongoing moral dialogue in which socialised individuals draw upon the very language and traditions they inherited in order to imagine freer, more humane ways of living together.'"
