"texte": [
"Across history, observers of political upheaval have marvelled—and shuddered—at how ordinarily gentle, reasonable individuals can suddenly participate in collective intolerance, scapegoating, and ideological extremism once swept up inside an impassioned crowd. Whether gathered physically in a feverish public square or assembled instantaneously across viral digital networks, collective formations possess a psychological dynamic qualitatively distinct from the sum of their individual members. As an observer of contemporary mass behaviour, I am convinced that understanding the mechanisms of crowd psychology and collective radicalisation has become an urgent democratic necessity in an era of algorithmic mobilisation.",
"In his pioneering 1895 treatise 'The Crowd: A Study of the Popular Mind', Gustave Le Bon identified three classical mechanisms through which physical gatherings transform individual judgement: 'anonymity', which lowers personal moral accountability; 'emotional contagion', whereby fear or anger spreads instantaneously like an epidemic; and 'suggestibility', which renders individuals receptive to simplistic, absolutist slogans that they would critically reject in solitude.",
"Decades later, in 'Crowds and Power', Elias Canetti deepened this phenomenology by analysing the intoxicating relief individuals experience when personal boundaries dissolve inside a unified mass: freed temporarily from everyday anxieties of status, isolation, and vulnerability, members of a 'baiting crowd' or 'closed ideological pack' derive exhilarating solidarity from targeting a common enemy—cementing group cohesion through shared hostility.",
"It is particularly wry to observe how twenty-first-century digital platforms have supercharged these nineteenth-century crowd dynamics while stripping away physical presence: sitting alone in quiet bedrooms behind glowing screens, millions of users experience the instantaneous anonymity, emotional contagion, and tribal euphoria of a roaring stadium mob—encouraged by engagement algorithms that systematically amplify moral outrage and punish hesitant moderation.",
"Empirical social psychology confirms this structural drift through what Cass Sunstein has termed the 'law of group polarisation': when like-minded individuals deliberate exclusively within insulated enclaves—whether partisan echo chambers or radicalised online forums—the group's collective position does not converge toward the moderate average, but shifts inexorably toward a more extreme version of its initial bias.",
"To dissect the pathologies of crowd psychology and collective radicalisation is not, of course, to condemn peaceful democratic mass assemblies, labour strikes, or civil-rights demonstrations. Throughout history, courageous collective mobilisation has been the indispensable engine of emancipation against tyranny and injustice.",
"Immunising democratic societies against destructive collective radicalisation therefore requires designing civic and digital environments that preserve collective solidarity while protecting individual critical judgement: regulating algorithmic amplification of viral outrage and anonymous bot swarms; cultivating cross-cutting public institutions—mixed schools, civic service, and deliberative town halls—where citizens encounter diverse neighbours face-to-face; and nurturing an educational ethic that prizes moral independence over herd applause."
