    "texte": [
      "Human beings are fundamentally relational creatures whose sense of worth never germinates in splendid isolation. From an infant's earliest search for a caregiver's approving gaze to an adult's longing for respect among peers and fellow citizens, the desire for recognition constitutes an indispensable engine of moral and social development. Yet when this healthy human need becomes unmoored from stable communities and harnessed to competitive status markets or algorithmic applause metrics, it mutates into an acute psychological vulnerability—leaving individuals perpetually anxious, easily manipulated, and dependent upon external validation for their basic sense of existence.",
      "In his 'Phenomenology of Spirit', Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel placed the struggle for mutual recognition at the very centre of human self-consciousness: a subject becomes genuinely free only when recognised by another free, equal subject whom it recognises in turn. Building upon Hegel's insight, the contemporary philosopher Axel Honneth has demonstrated in 'The Struggle for Recognition' that human flourishing depends upon three distinct spheres of reciprocal affirmation—intimate love, equal legal rights, and social esteem for one's concrete contributions to the common good.",
      "More than two centuries earlier, however, Jean-Jacques Rousseau had diagnosed the dark shadow of this social dependence in his 'Discourse on the Origin of Inequality'. Rousseau contrasted healthy 'amour de soi'—the natural instinct for self-preservation and quiet wellbeing—with feverish 'amour-propre', the comparative vanity that measures one's worth solely by outshining neighbours and winning public applause, condemning modern individuals to live perpetually outside themselves in the restless opinions of others.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe how contemporary social-media architectures have industrialised Rousseauian 'amour-propre' into a twenty-four-hour slot machine of intermittent validation: millions of citizens curate every meal, vacation, charitable gesture, and grief into public performances calibrated for algorithmic hearts and follower counts—experiencing a micro-spike of euphoria when applause surges and a quiet collapse of self-worth whenever engagement metrics dip.",
