"texte": [
"Convening at Cheikh Anta Diop University in Dakar for an international colloquium on philosophy, science, and education, the historian of African thought Dr Aminata Diallo and the philosopher of science Professor Henrik Lindholm confronted a profound intellectual question: what does it mean to 'decolonise knowledge' in universities, scientific research, and school curricula without surrendering rigorous empirical truth to relativism? 'For centuries, European imperial expansion rested not only upon military and economic supremacy, but upon an epistemic monopoly,' Dr Diallo opened. 'In "The Invention of Africa", the philosopher Valentin-Yves Mudimbe showed how colonial libraries, anthropology, and university curricula elevated provincial Western categories as the sole measure of "universal reason", while dismissing African philosophical traditions, ecological knowledge, and oral historiography as mere folklore or pre-logical myth.'",
"'You diagnose the historical arrogance of Eurocentric universalism with undeniable precision, Aminata, yet we must beware of falling into the opposite trap of cultural essentialism,' Professor Lindholm replied with measured nuance. 'As the Beninese philosopher Paulin Hountondji warned in "African Philosophy: Myth and Reality", treating African thought solely as a collective, timeless "ethnophilosophy" insulated from critical debate and scientific inquiry actually reproduces colonial stereotypes rather than emancipating African intellectual creativity.'",
"'I wholeheartedly endorse Hountondji's critique of static ethnophilosophy, Henrik, because living traditions thrive on internal debate, rational rigour, and empirical discovery,' Diallo countered, sharpening her epistemological argument. 'Decolonising knowledge never means rejecting mathematics, molecular biology, or critical historiography; it means exposing how global academic publishing, citation hierarchies, and research funding still marginalise scholars and languages outside the Global North.'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe the performative lip service paid to "global epistemic diversity" by prestigious Western universities today,' Lindholm remarked with a dry smile: 'academic conferences eagerly invite scholars from Africa, Asia, and Latin America to speak on panels about "local indigenous perspectives", while reserving theoretical authority, journal editorships, and multi-million-dollar research grants almost exclusively for institutions in London, Paris, and Boston.'",
"'That structural asymmetry illustrates why the sociologist Boaventura de Sousa Santos called for "epistemologies of the South" and "cognitive justice",' Diallo agreed warmly. 'True universality cannot be decreed unilaterally from a single imperial centre; it emerges only through horizontal dialogue among diverse knowledge traditions—integrating African agroecological wisdom, multilingual philosophical concepts, and rigorous empirical science.'",
"'Precisely!' Lindholm added, completing the synthesis. 'Just as medieval Islamic, Andalusian, and African scholars in Timbuktu and Fez preserved, critiqued, and expanded ancient Greek and Indian mathematics, contemporary science grows stronger when it draws upon the full intellectual repertoire of humanity.'",
"Aminata Diallo nodded in agreement: 'In short, decolonising knowledge is neither an anti-scientific retreat into dogma nor a superficial renaming of syllabi—it is the democratisation of reason itself, building a genuinely pluriversal republic of letters where every civilisation contributes on equal footing to understanding and healing our shared world.'"
