      "Across the global economy of the twenty-first century, international commerce is no longer organised primarily around simple exchanges of finished national products; instead, production is fractured across intricate 'global value chains' spanning research laboratories, component factories, logistics corridors, and retail branding hubs. Within this architecture, however, the African continent occupies a stubbornly subordinate position forged during the colonial era: despite supplying essential agricultural commodities and strategic minerals—cocoa, coffee, cotton, gold, cobalt, copper, and crude petroleum—African economies capture only a tiny fraction of final consumer value, remaining structurally trapped at the lowest-margin extraction tier of world trade.",
