"texte": [
"Convening at the Museum of Black Civilisations in Dakar for an international symposium on cultural heritage and historical justice, the art historian Dr Kwame Mensah and the European museum curator Dr Claire Beaumont confronted one of the most charged moral and diplomatic controversies of our era: should Western museums permanently return royal regalia, sacred sculptures, and historical bronzes looted during colonial expeditions to their African countries of origin, or preserve them within global 'encyclopaedic museums'? 'Roughly ninety percent of sub-Saharan Africa's material cultural heritage resides today outside the continent, locked inside European and North American museum vaults and private collections,' Dr Mensah began. 'When sacred objects such as the Benin Bronzes or royal Ethiopian manuscripts were seized at gunpoint during punitive military raids, keeping them abroad under the pretext of "universal stewardship" perpetuates an unresolved colonial injury and deprives young Africans of direct contact with their ancestors' artistic genius.'",
"'You state the moral gravity of colonial plunder with undeniable force, Kwame, yet museum scholars raise legitimate practical and philosophical questions about conservation capacity and global cultural dialogue,' Dr Beaumont replied with measured nuance. 'Thinkers such as Kwame Anthony Appiah have argued in "Cosmopolitanism" that great artworks belong simultaneously to their creators and to all humanity—and that encyclopaedic institutions in London, Paris, or New York allow millions of visitors from every continent to encounter African civilisation alongside Asian and European masterpieces.'",
"'I appreciate the ideal of cosmopolitan appreciation, Claire, provided it is reciprocal rather than a one-way street,' Mensah countered, sharpening his critique. 'Right now, a Parisian or Londoner can admire African royal art on a Sunday afternoon, whereas a Nigerian or Senegalese student must apply for a costly, often-denied visa simply to view their own ancestral heritage behind foreign glass.'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe the paternalistic excuses invoked by certain European museum directors when resisting restitution,' Beaumont remarked with a dry smile: 'institutions whose galleries remained largely silent while colonised societies were stripped of their sacred objects suddenly express agonising concern over whether African museums possess "adequate climate-control humidity sensors"—even though many of those masterpieces survived for five centuries in West African palaces before being looted.'",
"'That honest admission goes straight to the heart of the landmark 2018 report authored by Felwine Sarr and Bénédicte Savoy!' Mensah agreed warmly. 'Restitution is never merely a logistical transfer of crates; it is a foundational act of relational ethics—restoring historical truth, repairing memory, and enabling African societies to reintegrate living cultural objects into contemporary education and civic life.'",
"'Precisely!' Beaumont added, completing the synthesis. 'True cultural universalism begins only when ownership is restored: once looted artefacts are legally returned to African institutions, museums across Africa and Europe can build genuine, equal partnerships—featuring rotating exhibitions, joint conservation laboratories, and shared digital archives founded upon mutual consent rather than historical coercion.'",
"Kwame Mensah nodded in agreement: 'In short, the restitution of African cultural heritage is neither an act of cultural isolationism nor a diplomatic zero-sum loss—it is an indispensable gesture of historical justice that heals colonial wounds and inaugurates a truly reciprocal global dialogue among equals.'"
],
