"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Central Bank Governors and Economists, Ladies and Gentlemen, currency is never a mere neutral piece of paper or digital ledger entry; it is one of the supreme instruments of state sovereignty, economic planning, and democratic accountability. Through monetary policy, credit allocation, and exchange-rate management, a sovereign polity steers investment toward employment, shields citizens from external shocks, and finances long-term structural transformation. Yet across large parts of Africa today, monetary sovereignty remains constrained by two powerful, intersecting forces: inherited post-colonial monetary arrangements tied to external currencies on one hand, and the complex technical demands of continental economic integration under the African Continental Free Trade Area on the other.",
"For decades, countries operating within pegged currency zones linked to the European euro—and earlier the French franc—accepted rigid exchange-rate stability and low inflation at a steep developmental price: surrendering independent monetary policy, parking substantial foreign-exchange reserves in external treasuries, and enduring an overvalued exchange rate that penalised local agricultural and industrial exports while subsidising foreign imports.",
