"INTERVIEWER: Professor Amina Osei-Mensah, scholar of comparative public administration and African governance, over the past four decades, international lenders and domestic reformers alike have launched wave after wave of 'institutional modernisation' programmes across Africa—introducing digital tax portals, civil-service performance audits, anti-corruption commissions, and imported administrative codes. Yet citizens frequently complain that public services remain unresponsive or disconnected from everyday social reality. Why do technocratic modernisation reforms so often falter in African public institutions?",
"PROFESSOR OSEI-MENSAH: Because most institutional reforms treat governance as a purely technical plumbing problem—assuming you can copy an administrative statute or digital software package from Stockholm or Singapore and bolt it onto a post-colonial state without addressing deeper questions of historical legitimacy and social trust! As the Nigerian political sociologist Peter Ekeh famously demonstrated in his theory of the 'two publics', colonial rule fractured civic morality into a 'primordial public' of family and community solidarity—where moral obligation is deeply felt—and an alien 'civic public' inherited from the colonial state, widely perceived as an external apparatus to be navigated or extracted from rather than owned by citizens.",
