    "texte": [
      "For more than two hundred millennia, Homo sapiens survived upon the Earth precisely because human physical strength and technical reach remained sharply bounded: a Stone Age flint axe or a nineteenth-century steam locomotive could alter a local valley, yet never threaten the atmospheric chemistry, genetic integrity, or survival of the species as a whole. Over the past eight decades, however, humanity has crossed an unprecedented evolutionary threshold. Armed simultaneously with thermonuclear arsenals, fossil-fuelled planetary heating, synthetic pathogen engineering, and autonomous artificial intelligence, our species now wields godlike geological and biological power while remaining governed by tribal institutions and Stone Age emotional reflexes—raising the defining question of our epoch: can human civilisation survive its own technological power?",
      "In his prophetic 1956 treatise 'The Obsolescence of Man', the philosopher Günther Anders diagnosed this fatal asymmetry as the 'Promethean gap': humanity's capacity to manufacture and trigger catastrophic technologies has expanded infinitely faster than our imaginative and moral capacity to feel or comprehend the consequences of what we set in motion. A technician pressing a launch button or releasing an engineered algorithm experiences an abstract mechanical gesture completely divorced from the distant human devastation it unleashes.",
      "Decades later, contemplating the fragile blue dot of Earth photographed from deep space, the astronomer Carl Sagan framed this predicament as a cosmic rite of passage: every technological civilisation in the universe eventually reaches a perilous 'technological adolescence' in which its power to destroy itself matures faster than its institutional wisdom to restrain itself—and only those species that forge planetary solidarity survive into maturity.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe the geopolitical bravado with which contemporary superpowers manage these existential hazards today: locked in frantic arms races over hypersonic missiles, autonomous drones, and frontier artificial intelligence, national leaders solemnise every escalatory budget as 'essential for strategic deterrence'—behaving like passengers on a wooden ship competing to build larger flamethrowers in the cargo hold.",
      "As the philosopher Hans Jonas argued in 'The Imperative of Responsibility', classical ethics—concerned solely with immediate relations between neighbours in an unchanging nature—is wholly inadequate for an era of irreversible planetary power: survival now demands an 'ethics of the future' anchored in a 'heuristics of fear', giving decisive weight to worst-case civilisational warnings whenever irreversible survival stakes are at risk.",
