"texte": [
"Convening at the Royal Society in London for a public debate on science, history, and ethics, the historian of science Professor Alistair Finch and the moral philosopher Dr Samira Al-Mansoor confronted a foundational assumption of Enlightenment modernity: does steady progress in scientific knowledge and technical mastery naturally carry humanity toward greater moral enlightenment? 'Over the past three centuries, the expansion of empirical science has undeniably demolished countless cruel superstitions—ending witch trials, exposing pseudo-scientific racial hierarchies, curing epidemic plagues, and expanding human empathy across wider circles,' Professor Finch opened. 'As Enlightenment thinkers from Condorcet to contemporary advocates of rational humanism argue, habits of empirical evidence, critical inquiry, and universal scientific laws gradually discipline human prejudice and elevate moral civilisation.'",
"'You highlight the liberating potential of critical reason with undeniable eloquence, Alistair, yet twentieth-century history shattered any comforting belief that scientific sophistication automatically guarantees moral decency,' Dr Al-Mansoor replied with incisive gravity. 'In "Dialectic of Enlightenment", Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer demonstrated how pure "instrumental reason"—obsessed exclusively with calculating the most efficient technical means while remaining indifferent to ethical ends—enabled modern industrialised states to engineer chemical warfare, nuclear annihilation, and bureaucratic genocide with chilling scientific precision.'",
"'I do not dispute that instrumental science can be harnessed by monstrous regimes, Samira, which is precisely why scientific communities must defend open democratic institutions and human rights,' Finch countered, refining his historical stance. 'Yet surely scientific literacy still erodes dogmatic fanaticism by teaching citizens to test claims against reality rather than blind authority?'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe how effortlessly modern technological elites combine cutting-edge scientific literacy with archaic moral selfishness,' Al-Mansoor remarked with a dry smile: 'engineers capable of landing reusable rockets on ocean barges or training billion-parameter neural networks routinely deploy that very brilliance to design addictive gambling apps for teenagers, evade public taxes, or optimize surveillance tools for authoritarian regimes.'",
"'That distinction between technical brilliance and ethical wisdom points directly to the ancient Socratic insight that knowledge of how things work is never identical to wisdom about how we ought to live,' Finch conceded thoughtfully. 'Scientific progress expands our power and widens our sphere of moral responsibility, yet it cannot by itself generate compassion, justice, or reverence for human dignity.'",
"'Precisely!' Al-Mansoor added, completing the synthesis. 'Moral progress is never an automatic by-product of laboratory discoveries; it is a fragile, hard-won civic and philosophical achievement forged through democratic struggles, humanities education, empathetic literature, and institutional accountability.'",
"Alistair Finch nodded in agreement: 'In short, scientific progress and moral progress advance along distinct tracks—and humanity achieves genuine enlightenment only when we refuse to treat technical cleverness as a substitute for ethical conscience, uniting rigorous empirical inquiry with an equally demanding cultivation of justice, empathy, and wisdom.'"
