"texte": [
"Stepping out of a gleaming glass skyscraper in Seoul's Gangnam financial district into a quiet, centuries-old traditional pottery courtyard tucked behind a stone wall in Bukchon, the philosopher and essayist Dr Min-Jae Park paused beside a potter carefully shaping raw clay on a wooden wheel. Minutes earlier, he had walked past luxury boutiques displaying watches worth more than an annual salary, automated convenience stores stocked with every conceivable commodity, and towering residential complexes equipped with biometric security. Watching the potter's unhurried, attentive hands bring form out of silent earth, Min-Jae reflected upon one of the defining paradoxes of affluent modern societies: how can unprecedented material abundance coexist so effortlessly with profound existential poverty?",
"In his classic 1976 study 'To Have or to Be?', the psychoanalyst and social philosopher Erich Fromm diagnosed the spiritual impoverishment hidden inside consumer affluence: modern industrial society trains individuals to orient their entire existence around the 'having mode'—measuring personal worth by accumulated possessions, credentials, and curated status symbols—while atrophying the 'being mode', which consists in active presence, creative craftsmanship, disinterested love, and solidarity with others.",
"Equally penetrating is the psychiatrist Viktor Frankl's concept of the 'existential vacuum': when material survival is secured without a compelling ethical purpose, meaningful vocation, or deep community bonds, affluent individuals frequently succumb to chronic boredom, compulsive distraction, and quiet despair—discovering that unlimited purchasing power cannot buy a reason to live.",
"It is particularly wry to observe how consumer capitalism monetises this very existential emptiness today: having stripped modern work of autonomy and hollowed out neighbourhood community life, the marketplace cheerfully sells anxious consumers expensive 'mindfulness retreats', minimalist luxury furnishings, and algorithmic self-help subscriptions—promising to cure spiritual poverty through yet another round of shopping.",
