"texte": [
"Watching great powers stumble once again into hubristic military escalations, speculative financial bubbles, ecological overshoot, and demagogic scapegoating—patterns documented across centuries of chronicles from ancient Athens to the twentieth century—an observer of human affairs can scarcely avoid a haunting question: is humanity condemned by some immutable flaw in its nature to repeat the catastrophic mistakes of its own history? Between the fatalistic cynicism that declares 'nothing ever changes' and the naive progressive optimism that assumes modern societies have outgrown ancestral follies lies a far more demanding truth: history never repeats itself mechanically like clockwork, yet human societies repeatedly re-enact familiar tragedies whenever institutional memory erodes and arrogance eclipses historical humility.",
"In his 'History of the Peloponnesian War', Thucydides famously wrote that he composed his account not as a transient prize essay, but as 'a possession for all time'—because so long as human nature remains constant, similar combinations of fear, honour, and self-interest will continue generating recognisably analogous political crises across epochs.",
"Centuries later, the philosopher George Santayana distilled this insight into one of modernity's most frequently quoted—and most frequently ignored—aphorisms: 'Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it.' Yet history repeats itself not merely because people forget dates and treaties, but because each generation flatters itself that its own superior technology or moral purity exempts it from the structural traps that ensnared its predecessors.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the chronological snobbery with which contemporary elites dismiss historical warnings today: surrounded by high-speed fiber networks, artificial intelligence models, and algorithmic trading screens, financiers and strategists routinely brush aside lessons from 1914 or 1929 with the breezy assurance that 'this time is different'—right up until the exact same dynamics of hubris, debt leverage, and miscalculation detonate on schedule.",
"At the same time, as the philosopher Karl Popper argued in 'The Poverty of Historicism', human history is never governed by ironclad, inescapable laws of destiny: unlike planetary orbits, social trajectories are shaped by human choices, institutional reforms, and moral courage—proven whenever societies actually broke cycles of ruin, as post-1945 Europe overcame centuries of Franco-German warfare through binding economic and democratic integration.",
"To question whether humanity is condemned to repeat its past mistakes is therefore neither to indulge in deterministic despair nor to pretend that historical knowledge alone automatically immunises us against folly. Knowing history does not prevent error unless that knowledge is embedded inside durable democratic institutions.",
"Breaking the cycle of historical repetition therefore requires translating historical memory into structural wisdom: protecting independent historical research and honest public education that confront national mistakes without censorship; building institutional checks—constitutional separation of powers, financial regulation, and arms-control treaties—designed precisely to restrain predictable human hubris; and cultivating civic humility so each generation learns from its ancestors' scars rather than insisting upon repeating them firsthand."
],
