"texte": [
"Convening at the University of Oxford for a public symposium on epistemology and democratic culture, the philosopher of knowledge Professor Eleanor Vance and the sociologist of media Dr Tariq Benjelloun confronted one of the defining cultural crises of the twenty-first century: can factual and scientific truth retain public authority in a fragmented media ecosystem where millions inhabit personalised 'subjective realities' validated by tribal echo chambers? 'In her prescient 1967 essay "Truth and Politics", Hannah Arendt warned that factual truth—unlike philosophical speculation—is acutely fragile because it depends upon human testimony, archives, and witnesses,' Professor Vance opened. 'When modern propaganda and algorithmic echo chambers blur the boundary between verified evidence and partisan opinion, citizens lose the common factual ground required for any meaningful political debate.'",
"'You diagnose the fragility of factual truth with undeniable clarity, Eleanor, yet we must also understand why appeals to "objective truth" have lost credibility among many disaffected citizens,' Dr Benjelloun replied with sociological nuance. 'For decades, powerful technocratic and media elites routinely wrapped partisan economic policies and flawed geopolitical interventions in the mantle of "unquestionable scientific objectivity"—provoking a populist backlash that now distrusts even genuine empirical science.'",
"'That distinction between genuine empirical objectivity and technocratic arrogance is vital, Tariq, yet abandoning the standard of truth altogether would be catastrophic,' Vance countered, sharpening her philosophical argument. 'As the philosopher Bernard Williams argued in "Truth and Truthfulness", a society that treats truth as nothing more than an ideological power-play inevitably hands victory to the loudest demagogue and the wealthiest propagandist—leaving the weak with no factual appeal against tyranny.'",
"'It is particularly wry to observe the selective relativism practised by contemporary "alternative-fact" entrepreneurs,' Benjelloun remarked with a dry smile: 'commentators who airily dismiss climate physics, epidemiological data, or historical archives as "merely one subjective narrative among others" suddenly become fierce, uncompromising empirical realists the instant someone misreports their own bank balance or television ratings.'",
"'That comic inconsistency exposes the bad faith behind radical relativism!' Vance agreed warmly. 'At the same time, as the philosopher and sociologist Bruno Latour argued late in his career, defending scientific truth does not mean pretending facts fall finished from the sky; it means making visible the transparent institutions, peer-review laboratories, independent archives, and rigorous methods that earn our warranted trust.'",
"'Precisely!' Benjelloun added, completing the synthesis. 'Truth regains public authority not through condescending decrees from distant authorities, but when citizens can inspect the evidence, see conflicts of interest disclosed openly, and participate in trustworthy civic institutions—public libraries, independent journalism, and open-science repositories—anchored in shared standards of verification.'",
"Eleanor Vance nodded in agreement: 'In short, truth cannot survive by authoritarian fiat nor dissolve into subjective whim; it retains its authority only when democratic societies vigorously defend both the humility of open inquiry and the institutional integrity of shared factual reality.'"
