    "texte": [
      "For nearly all of human history, the moral horizon of political and economic decision-making extended scarcely beyond the immediate neighbourhood and the living generation. Whether a ruler built an aqueduct, cleared a forest, or negotiated a treaty, the consequences unfolded within human lifetimes and remained largely reversible by subsequent generations. Today, however, humanity wields unprecedented capacities whose reverberations stretch across centuries and geological epochs: radioactive nuclear waste requiring ten-thousand-year containment, atmospheric greenhouse gases destabilising planetary climate for millennia, species extinctions erasing four billion years of evolution permanently, and heritable germline gene editing altering the human biological inheritance forever. Confronted with irreversible choices made in the present, modern societies face an acute ethical and constitutional crisis: how can democratic institutions represent the rights of unborn future generations who possess no ballot, no lobbyist, and no voice in today's assemblies?",
      "In 'The Imperative of Responsibility', the philosopher Hans Jonas formulated the foundational moral law for an age of irreversible technological reach: 'Act so that the effects of your action are compatible with the permanence of genuine human life on Earth.' Jonas demonstrated that traditional ethics—built around reciprocal duties among contemporaries—breaks down when present generations can unilaterally inflict irreversible ruin upon descendants who cannot yet protest or retaliate.",
      "Building upon this ethical insight in international law, the legal scholar Edith Brown Weiss articulated the doctrine of 'intergenerational equity' and 'planetary trust': every living generation holds the Earth not as an absolute private owner free to liquidate its capital, but as a temporary fiduciary trustee obligated to pass the natural and cultural patrimony onward in no worse condition than it was received.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe the institutional short-termism built into modern electoral and financial calendars today: politicians obsessed with the next two-year election cycle and corporate executives compensated on quarterly earnings reports routinely mortgage the climatic stability of the twenty-second century in order to subsidise a marginal uptick in this quarter's GDP—solemnly defending their reckless borrowing from the future as 'fiscal prudence'.",
      "As the philosopher Roman Krznaric has argued in 'The Good Ancestor', contemporary democracies suffer from a structural 'tyranny of the present' that colonises the future as an unpopulated territory devoid of legal rights—dumping ecological debt, toxic waste, and depleted aquifers onto unborn children with zero representation.",
      "To champion our responsibility toward future generations is not, of course, to ignore acute poverty, hunger, or injustice among living people today. Intergenerational justice and intragenerational justice are inseparable twins: a society that abandons impoverished citizens in the present will never muster the moral solidarity required to protect descendants in the future.",
