"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Scholars, Citizens and Youth of the World, Ladies and Gentlemen, standing at the crossroads of an extraordinary and perilous century—defined simultaneously by breathtaking scientific ingenuity, converging ecological emergencies, deep social inequalities, and profound transformations in human consciousness—we confront the supreme civilisational question of our age: can humanity move beyond reactive crisis management to envision and construct a civilisation founded deliberately upon intellectual clarity, social and planetary justice, and universal responsibility? To dismiss such an aspiration as naive utopianism is to forget that every enduring democratic institution—constitutional rights, universal public education, international law, and abolition of slavery—began as an audacious moral vision long before becoming historical reality.",
"First, a civilisation worthy of human dignity requires 'intellectual clarity': the courage to pierce through ideological fog, algorithmic manipulation, and comforting self-deception in order to perceive reality honestly. As Hannah Arendt reminded us, freedom begins with the willingness to think without banisters—examining empirical evidence rigorously, naming injustice without euphemism, and refusing to surrender our critical judgement to demagogues or machines.",
"Second, clarity without solidarity remains sterile unless embodied in 'justice'—not merely formal legal symmetry on paper, but substantive social, economic, and ecological justice across every scale of human life. As John Rawls and Amartya Sen illuminated from complementary perspectives, a just civilisation is measured by how it uplifts its most vulnerable members, expands real human capabilities for every child regardless of birthplace, and honours historical equity between the Global North and the Global South.",
"It is particularly wry to observe how often contemporary power-holders praise 'clarity, justice, and responsibility' as decorative ornaments in ceremonial speeches while treating them as impractical inconveniences the moment concrete budgets, tax treaties, or climate targets are negotiated—as though civilisational ideals were fine porcelain to be admired behind glass rather than sturdy tools meant for daily public life.",
"Third, in an interdependent biosphere where local actions reverberate across oceans and centuries, clarity and justice culminate in 'universal responsibility': an ethic embracing our fellow citizens across borders, our unborn descendants across future generations, and the millions of non-human species with whom we share this living Earth.",
"Envisioning a civilisation founded on clarity, justice, and universal responsibility does not mean, Ladies and Gentlemen, dreaming of a uniform, conflict-free utopia that erases cultural plurality or human imperfection. Pluralism, debate, and fragile striving belong permanently to the human condition; our task is to build institutions wise enough to channel human creativity toward life rather than ruin.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call upon each of us—scientists and poets, educators and workers, elders and youth—to become conscious co-architects of this civilisational renewal: defending truth and critical education as sacred public goods; rebuilding economies around human flourishing, fair redistribution, and ecological regeneration; strengthening democratic multilateralism grounded in equal dignity for all peoples; and proving by our daily courage that humanity is capable not merely of surviving its own power, but of inaugurating a wiser, gentler, and more just epoch upon this shared Earth."
